{"name":"Kangaroo","hex":"#c5c3b0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kangaroo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the Macropodidae family of large marsupials with strong hind legs for hopping, native to Australia and New Guinea. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A hooded jacket with a front pocket, usually of fleece material, a kangaroo jacket."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To practice kangaroo care on an infant; to hold a premature infant against the skin.","To hunt kangaroo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kangaroo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kangaroo","blocks":[{"text":"Kangaroos are marsupials from the subfamily Macropodinae. In common use, the term is used to describe the largest species from this family, the red kangaroo, as well as the antilopine kangaroo, eastern grey kangaroo, and western grey kangaroo. Kangaroos are indigenous to Australia and New Guinea. The Australian government estimated that 42.8 million kangaroos lived within the commercial harvest areas of Australia in 2019, down from 53.2 million in 2013."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Forester_kangaroo_%28Macropus_giganteus_tasmaniensis%29_juvenile_hopping_Esk_Valley.jpg/330px-Forester_kangaroo_%28Macropus_giganteus_tasmaniensis%29_juvenile_hopping_Esk_Valley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangaroo","wikidata":"Q5070208"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/RedRoo.JPG/500px-RedRoo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/RedRoo.JPG/500px-RedRoo.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A male red kangaroo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARedRoo.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3